 "footnotes": [
  {
   "n": 1,
   "title": "Dependent",
   "text": "After a word that already sets the time: *walâ* 'did not' above all, or a time word (*Úgmà níya palitá* 'He will buy it tomorrow'). *Dílì* takes the irrealis instead."
  },
  {
   "n": 2,
   "title": "Imperative",
   "text": "Patient and circumstance borrow the dependent (`-a`, `-i`; Wolff); the instrument keeps `i-`. In the agent, `pag-` is optional in the perfective, where the bare base sounds more forceful (Wolff, course 11C1), and required in the progressive (Raisanen & Raisanen, *A Handbook of Cebuano Visayan*, 2016; Bunye & Yap). After *ayáw* 'don't', `pag-` is usual; a plain irrealis makes a softer suggestion (Tanangkingsing, ch. 9)."
  },
  {
   "n": 3,
   "title": "Volitional, potential",
   "text": "Wikipedia's moods, indicative and potential. \"Volitional\" follows Tanangkingsing (Wolff: nonpotential), since commands sit on that side. The potential has no imperative and one set for both aspects. Wikipedia's third mood, the mirative (`nahi-`, `mahi-`), is Wolff's `hi-` 'accidental', under All affixes."
  },
  {
   "n": 4,
   "title": "Distributive",
   "text": "Many doers, many things, or again and again. Wolff's derived base `paN-` (§6.2) under the ordinary affixes (`mo-` + `paN-` = `maN-`); he also has a progressive, `magpaN-`, `nagpaN-`. The command is `paN-` or `pagpaN-` (Wolff, course 11C3a). The dashed dependents of the distributive potential (`mapaN-`, `mapaN-…-i`) are built by Wolff's rule that a `paN-` base takes the ordinary affixes; no source prints them."
  },
  {
   "n": 5,
   "title": "Stative",
   "text": "No doer, so no trigger. The state itself (`ma-` on *túlog* 'sleep') is the potential's affix: Wolff has one `ma-` entry for both. The volitional side holds the agent's affixes as they read on a verb of state (Wolff §7.11-7.12). The two sides pair by shape only, so read each side's gloss. Wikipedia's passive statives (`gika-`, `ka-…-an`, `ika-`) are Wolff's alternates of the potential and stand there, mostly with verbs of feeling (*hádlok* 'fear'). *Palít* has no stative: *mapalít* is 'can be bought'."
  },
  {
   "n": 6,
   "title": "Literary or archaic",
   "text": "The progressive passives (`gina-`, `paga-…-on`, `iga-`) are literary outside Southern Leyte and Bohol (Wolff); in speech the perfective passives cover both meanings. `naga-` is archaic (Raisanen & Raisanen)."
  },
